/*
 * **Engineering options** — which blueprints and which experimental effects each
 * outfitting module can actually take.
 *
 * Sources: EDSY (github.com/taleden/EDSY) eddb.js — its module-group tables carry the
 *          `blueprints` and `expeffects` a group offers and which modules belong to it,
 *          and its module records carry the per-module `noblueprints` / `noexpeffects`
 *          denials that narrow either list. EDSY is (c) taleden under a CC BY-NC 4.0
 *          License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). coriolis-data
 *          (github.com/EDCD/coriolis-data) modifications/modules.json — the same per-group
 *          lists keyed by the journal `BlueprintName`s this catalogue joins on, which is
 *          what settles the families EDSY records under one generic id, and which of the
 *          two Overcharged recipes the multi-cannons take. coriolis-data is (c) EDCD under
 *          the MIT License. Four rows come from neither registry: the Operations Merc-Coin
 *          blueprints `FuelScoop_Efficiency` and the three lasers'
 *          `PulseLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion`, `BurstLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion`
 *          and `BeamLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion`, whose module family comes from the
 *          Inara blueprint registry
 *          (https://inara.cz/elite/blueprints/) — the same source the recipes themselves
 *          came from. Neither EDSY nor coriolis-data publishes the Operations family at
 *          all. See SOURCES.md for how that read was obtained and what it was corroborated
 *          against. The underlying game logic is Elite Dangerous data, property of
 *          Frontier Developments plc, under Frontier's media-usage terms:
 *          https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * Availability is a property of the **module**, not of the blueprint: a Pulse Laser and
 * a Rail Gun share the Efficient blueprint but offer different experimental effects. So
 * modules are grouped (`modules` maps a module symbol to a `groups` key) and each group
 * lists the `blueprints` and `experimentals` it offers. Both lists hold ids that join
 * straight to `blueprints.jsonc` and `experimental-effects.jsonc`.
 *
 * **A group is one menu, so modules of the same kind with different menus are different
 * groups.** Upstream denies blueprints per module, and where those denials split a family
 * in two the split is stored as two groups rather than as an exception: a Guardian Power
 * Plant takes Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance and none of the ordinary power-plant
 * blueprints, an ordinary one the reverse, so `powerPlants` and `guardianPowerPlants` are
 * separate — as are the distributors and the hull reinforcement packages. The Guardian
 * half of each pair also takes **no experimental effect**: Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance
 * is its whole menu and that recipe has no experimental slot, so its `experimentals` are
 * empty where its ordinary twin's are not. The same holds for the other Guardian module
 * families — FSD boosters, module and shield reinforcement — which were already empty.
 * Guardian *weapons* are the same shape: `guardianGauss`, `guardianPlasma` and
 * `guardianShard` list that one recipe too. An ordinary weapon recipe on a Guardian weapon
 * is always a **purchase**, never an engineer roll — see §Engineering options in
 * SOURCES.md — so `pre-engineered.jsonc` carries it and no menu here does. An engineered
 * Guardian module that does carry an experimental was obtained already engineered — a
 * community-goal or tech-broker reward — rather than rolled at an engineer; no registry
 * publishes those variants and `pre-engineered.jsonc` does not carry them, so this
 * catalogue's answer is about the engineering menu only. Neither registry publishes the
 * distinction either (`expeffects` is per module group, with no per-module denial on any
 * of them), so SOURCES.md records where it came from.
 *
 * **A menu lists the id of the recipe it rolls, which is not always the id the game
 * writes.** The three utility-scanner menus list `Scanner_LongRange` / `Scanner_WideAngle`,
 * but the game writes `Sensor_LongRange` / `Sensor_WideAngle` on a scanner — the same ids
 * it writes on a sensor suite, whose Long Range and Wide Angle are different recipes
 * rolling different stats in different directions (Long Range costs the suite mass and
 * the scanner power draw; Wide Angle the reverse). Nothing is stored here for that: the
 * journal spelling belongs to the recipe, so it lives on the blueprint record as
 * `journalName` in `blueprints.jsonc`. What this file contributes is the half only it
 * knows — which of the two recipes a given module is offered. `resolveBlueprintForModule`
 * reads a menu against those journal names, so resolution needs no second list to drift
 * from this one. The third id of that shape is coriolis-data's `MC_Overcharged`, the
 * Overcharged that also cuts the clip by 3–15% where the game writes the
 * `Weapon_Overcharged` every other weapon menu lists; `multiCannons` and
 * `antiXenoMultiCannons` list it. The anti-xeno menu takes it on EDSY's word rather than
 * coriolis's, which describes no anti-xeno group at all — see §Multi-cannon Overcharged
 * in SOURCES.md.
 *
 * **A menu may list an Operations recipe.** Most Operations ids belong to modules bought
 * already engineered and no menu names them, but four are recipes a player applies: the
 * Merc-Coin blueprints published with a full grade 1–5 — `FuelScoop_Efficiency` on the
 * fuel scoops and the three lasers' `PulseLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion`,
 * `BurstLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion` and
 * `BeamLaser_ThermalPlasmaConversion`. (Anti-Guardian Zone Resistance is a fifth recipe of
 * that kind, listed by the nine Guardian groups under the journal id
 * `GuardianModule_Sturdy` rather than under an Operations key.) A purchased
 * pre-engineered module's recipe starts at grade 2 because grade 1 is what the purchase
 * contains; these four start at grade 1, so nothing is pre-applied and the whole recipe is
 * rolled at an engineer. See "Four Operations recipes are listed by a menu" in SOURCES.md.
 *
 * `exclusions` handles what is left: the modules that take their group's blueprints but
 * not all of its experimental effects — a Multi-cannon cannot take Phasing Sequence, and
 * the Abrasion Blaster takes no experimental at all. Each entry lists the effect ids that
 * module cannot take, already expanded — subtract it from the group's `experimentals` to
 * get the exact set. Modules absent from `exclusions` take their whole group's list.
 *
 * Only modules the game lets you engineer appear at all; everything else is absent. That
 * is 1028 of the 1197 modules. The other 169 are the families no blueprint reaches — fuel
 * tanks, passenger cabins, the repair, recon, research, decontamination and multi-limpet
 * controllers, meta-alloy hull reinforcement, the plain module reinforcement packages,
 * the Pulse Wave Analyser, the mining launchers, Shock Cannons, Nanite Torpedo Pylons,
 * fighter and vehicle hangars, docking computers and Supercruise Assist, the module
 * stabilisers, the planetary approach suites, the discovery scanners, the cargo hatch and
 * the AX utility modules — plus the individual modules upstream denies every blueprint:
 * every anti-xeno multi-cannon but the two gimballed, both Enhanced anti-xeno missile
 * racks and every turreted plain one, five of the seven mining tools, the remote-release
 * launchers (three denied outright, the fourth left with only the dormant Decorative
 * entries) and the Mk II Plasma Shock Autocannon.
 *
 * `modules` is ordered by group, in the order `groups` declares them, and within a group
 * in module-catalogue order.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md.
 */{
